<section class="alt need"><div class="wrap">
<h2>Why it matters</h2>
<p class="sub">Iron deficiency and the emotional strain of pregnancy are common, serious, and often overlooked.</p>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card"><div class="big">37%</div><p>of pregnant women worldwide had anaemia in 2019. Iron deficiency is its most common nutritional cause.<a class="ref" href="about.html#src1" aria-label="Source 1">[1]</a></p></div>
<div class="card"><div class="big">1 in 4</div><p>pregnant women in the Philippines had anaemia in 2023.<a class="ref" href="about.html#src2" aria-label="Source 2">[2]</a></p></div>
<div class="card"><div class="big">~70%</div><p>fewer cases of anaemia at the end of pregnancy among women taking daily iron, in a review of clinical trials.<a class="ref" href="about.html#src3" aria-label="Source 3">[3]</a></p></div>
<div class="card"><div class="big">1 in 5</div><p>new mothers in low- and lower-middle-income countries experience a common mental disorder, such as depression or anxiety, after childbirth.<a class="ref" href="about.html#src5" aria-label="Source 5">[5]</a></p></div>
</div>
<p class="sub after-grid">The World Health Organization recommends daily iron and folic acid for every pregnant woman.<a class="ref" href="about.html#src4" aria-label="Source 4">[4]</a> <a href="about.html#sources">See all sources</a></p>
</div></section>

<section><div class="wrap">
<h2>What one kit holds</h2>
<p class="sub">Every mother in a cycle receives the same complete kit during her prenatal care.</p>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card"><h3>Iron (ferrous) tablets</h3><p>Bought locally by the clinic's staff with funds we send. Iron supports the normal growth and development of her baby and helps her feel less tired.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Two health guides</h3><p>One on taking ferrous tablets, one on mental health in pregnancy and new motherhood. Written in Tagalog and English and checked by the clinic's medical team before printing.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>A handwritten card</h3><p>Written by a student volunteer in the U.S. with a word of encouragement and a verse or prayer for each mother.</p></div>
</div>
</div></section>

